Washington, D.C. – Today, the House Armed Services Subcommittee on Readiness, of which Congressman Joe Wilson (R-SC) is the Chairman, published their proposals for tomorrow's subcommittee markup hearing.

The Readiness Subcommittee's proposal:

Rebuilds troop readiness and makes investments in equipment maintenance by:

Recommending increased funding for equipment maintenance, spare parts, and training to rebuild readiness for ships, aircraft squadrons, and ground combat units;

Recommending the Full Committee fund 20 Combat Training Center rotations across the Army—16 Active and 4 Army National Guard;

Requiring the Navy's Board of Inspection and Survey Inspections be conducted on a no-notice basis and make details of those inspections unclassified and available to the public;

Requiring the Secretary of the Navy to provide a report on ways to optimize surface Navy vessel inspection and crew certifications to reduce redundancies and the burden on inspection visits that ships undergo;

Limiting the amount of time a Navy vessel is forward deployed overseas to no more than 10 years;

Ensuring the Navy retains sufficient ship repair capability and capacity in the Western Pacific by prohibiting the redevelopment of the Former Ship Repair Facility on Guam.

Ensures infrastructure supports military readiness by:

Recommending the Full Committee increase funds for facilitysustainment above the fiscal year 2019 request;

Establishing a new Defense Community Infrastructure Program which will provide for resilient facilities and utilities off-base that directly supporting our military installations;

Authorizes the Secretary of the military departments to carry out military construction projects to enhance force protection and safety on military installations;

Extends the Defense Laboratory Modernization Pilot Program until October 1, 2023, and clarifies that the authority can be used for architectural and engineering services in addition to construction of infrastructure projects.

Improves oversight by:

Requiring the Department of Defense to include cyber and space in its quarterly readiness report to better reflect the National Defense Strategy and to ensure a comprehensive look at readiness across every domain of warfare;

Directing several assessments of the military departments' plans to rebuild readiness, enhance exercises, and modernize training requirements.

The Readiness Subcommittee markup will be live streamed on the House Armed Services Committee's website starting at 9 a.m. tomorrow.

WASHINGTON  In response to reports that Environmental Protection Agency Administrator Scott Pruitt was moving to consolidate the National Center for Environmental Research (NCER) with other offices, Congressman A. Donald McEachin (VA-04) introduced the Healthy Environment for Children Act to prohibit any reduction, consolidation, or termination of NCER or its programs. NCER supports research to improve public health, with a particular focus on children.
Reducing, consolidating or terminating NCER would significantly weaken the EPAs effectiveness in protecting human health and our environment. By preventing such changes, the Healthy Environment for Children Act would safeguard research into:
 protecting the health of children and adults from chemical exposure;
	reducing childrens health risks from environmental factors;
	air pollution, climate change, and air quality as they affect children and adults, including expecting mothers and newborns;
	water quality and availability;
	potential water contaminants that could affect childrens health;
	safer use of chemicals in consumer products;
	protection of vulnerable ecosystems; and
	means of achieving greater sustainability and harmony between humans and nature.
 The bill would also protect related initiatives, including work at the Childrens Environmental Health and Disease Prevention Research Center.
Our children deserve better than the toxic policies that Administrator Pruitt and President Trump are enacting, said Congressman Donald McEachin. Their continued assault on science-based research and efforts to protect childrens health is deeply harmful, and consolidating NCER is simply the latest attack on those fundamental safeguards. In the spirit of Earth Day and the upcoming Air Quality Awareness Week, I introduced the Healthy Environment for Children Act to protect our children and our childrens children. They deserve the clean air, water, and land that only science-based policies and a strong EPA can deliver.
Administrator Pruitt has consistently challenged the EPAs long tradition of science-based decision-making. Just yesterday, Pruitt announced a proposed rule that would greatly reduce the EPAs ability to use high-quality data from important scientific studies in the false guise of promoting transparency. Congressman McEachin knows that if we reject or ignore what science tells us, we will not make the best environmental decisions for future generations.
Congressman McEachin has previously introduced other legislation to protect clean air and water for future generations. Last year, McEachin introduced the House version of the bicameral Pollution Transparency Act, which would require agencies to use consistent, scientific, evidence-based metrics to measure the true impact of greenhouse gas emissions in cost/benefit-based decision-making.
